### Summary

CVE-2026-30973 [(GHSA-rfx7-4xw3-gh4m)](https://github.com/appium/appium/security/advisories/GHSA-rfx7-4xw3-gh4m) was a plain path traversal in the entry name. The containment check built an Error object without throwing it, so an entry literally named something like `../evil.txt` was written outside the destination. That issue was fixed in @appium/support 7.0.6 by actually rejecting out of bound entry names.

This finding is a different vector that the 7.0.6 fix does not cover. The archive entry names here are completely legitimate (for example `pwn` and `pwn/owned.txt`, no `..` anywhere), so every name based defense accepts them. The escape happens through a symbolic link. One entry is a symlink whose target points outside the extraction directory, and a later entry is a regular file whose name resolves through that symlink. The name based containment check never inspects the symlink target, and the extractor writes the symlink target verbatim, so the file lands outside the extraction root.

The two issues compared on the same patched version 7.2.5:

| | CVE-2026-30973 | This finding |
|---|---|---|
| Vector | one entry whose name contains `..` | a symlink entry plus a file written through it |
| Entry names | contain `..` (illegal) | legitimate, no `..` and not absolute |
| What is missing | the `..` name chec\k did not throw | name checks never resolve symlinks, and the symlink target is unchecked |
| Entries needed | one | two |
| Fixed in 7.0.6 | yes | no |
| Status on 7.2.5 | blocked | still exploitable (proven below) |

### Details

## Root cause

The extractor applies only a lexical containment check on each entry's own name, then for a symlink entry it creates the link with the target taken straight from the archive:

```js
// @appium/support build/lib/zip.js (extractAllTo path), version 7.2.5
97   if (!isSubPath(dest, dir)) {                 // lexical only, never resolves symlinks
98       throw new Error(`Out of bound path "${dest}" found while processing file ${fileName}`);
99   }
...
142  await fs.symlink(link, dest);                // link target comes verbatim from the archive
```

`isSubPath` compares normalized strings and does not call realpath, so for the file entry `pwn/owned.txt` the destination `dir/pwn/owned.txt` is lexically inside `dir` and the check passes, even though `dir/pwn` is a symlink created by the previous entry that points outside `dir`. The write then follows that symlink and escapes. The symlink target itself is never validated against the extraction root.

